# apps/web/.dockerignore
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE: the web image is built from the REPO ROOT
# (`docker build -f apps/web/Dockerfile .`) because apps/web is an npm
# workspace and needs the root package.json + package-lock.json. In that case
# Docker reads /.dockerignore, not this file. This file still matters for:
#   * tools that build with apps/web as the context,
#   * anyone who runs `docker build ./apps/web` by habit,
#   * BuildKit's per-Dockerfile ignore lookup (apps/web/Dockerfile.dockerignore
#     conventions in some CI images),
# so it is kept in sync with the root one on purpose. Belt and braces.
#
# SECURITY, not optimisation: the .env rules below exist because a file copied
# into the build context can be baked into an image layer, and image layers are
# permanently readable via `docker history` / `docker save` even if a later
# layer deletes the file. A published image containing a real .env is a
# credential-disclosure incident. Secrets are supplied at run time only.

# --- Dependencies -----------------------------------------------------------
node_modules
.npm

# --- Build output -----------------------------------------------------------
.next
out
dist
build
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# --- Python leftovers (if anyone vendors a venv here) -----------------------
.venv
venv
__pycache__
*.py[cod]

# --- SECURITY: never ship environment files (see header) --------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- VCS / CI / editor ------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

# --- Tests, reports, logs ---------------------------------------------------
tests
test-results
playwright-report
playwright.config.ts
vitest.config.ts
coverage
*.log

# --- Docs and prose ---------------------------------------------------------
docs
*.md

# --- Other deploy targets ---------------------------------------------------
.vercel
